{"name":"Pyrite Slate Green","hex":"#867452","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pyrite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The common mineral iron disulfide (FeS2), of a pale brass-yellow color and brilliant metallic luster, crystallizing in the isometric system.","(usually as a plural: pyrites) Any metallic-looking sulphide, such as the above, which is the most common."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pyrite"},{"term":"slate","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flake or piece of certain types of stone that tend to cleave into thin layers.\n A piece of such stone, usually cut into a rectangular shape, used as a tile for flooring, roofing, etc.;  such tiles collectively, or the material from which they are made.\n\nA piece of other material used as a roofing tile.","A piece of such stone, usually cut into a rectangular shape, used as a tile for flooring, roofing, etc.;  such tiles collectively, or the material from which they are made."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the bluish-grey colour of slate (noun etymology 1, noun sense 4)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/slate"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pyrite","blocks":[{"text":"The mineral pyrite ( PY-ryte), or iron pyrite, also known as fool's gold, is an iron sulfide with the chemical formula FeS2 (iron (II) disulfide). Pyrite is the most abundant sulfide mineral."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Pyrite_-_Huanzala_mine%2C_Huallanca%2C_Bolognesi%2C_Ancash%2C_Peru.jpg/330px-Pyrite_-_Huanzala_mine%2C_Huallanca%2C_Bolognesi%2C_Ancash%2C_Peru.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyrite","wikidata":"Q50769"},{"title":"Slate","blocks":[{"text":"Slate is a fine-grained, foliated, homogeneous, metamorphic rock derived from an original shale-type sedimentary rock composed of clay or volcanic ash through low-grade, regional metamorphism. It is the finest-grained foliated metamorphic rock. Foliation may not correspond to the original sedimentary layering, but instead is in planes perpendicular to the direction of metamorphic compression."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Slate_%28Knife_Lake_Formation%2C_metamorphism_at_2.7_Ga%2C_Neoarchean%3B_Rt._135_roadcut%2C_Gilbert%2C_Minnesota%2C_USA%29_3_%2823140002749%29.jpg/330px-Slate_%28Knife_Lake_Formation%2C_metamorphism_at_2.7_Ga%2C_Neoarchean%3B_Rt._135_roadcut%2C_Gilbert%2C_Minnesota%2C_USA%29_3_%2823140002749%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slate","wikidata":"Q207079"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"},{"title":"Slate gray","blocks":[{"text":"Slate gray is a moderate shade of gray with a slight azure tinge, which represents the average color of slate, a metamorphic rock often used in stonemasonry. As a tertiary color, slate is an equal mix of purple and green pigments."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/St_Fagans_Tannery_7.jpg/330px-St_Fagans_Tannery_7.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slate_gray","wikidata":"Q1411452"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/2780M-pyrite1.jpg/500px-2780M-pyrite1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/2780M-pyrite1.jpg/1280px-2780M-pyrite1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/2780M-pyrite1.jpg/500px-2780M-pyrite1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pyrite cubic crystals on marl from Navajún, La Rioja, Spain (size: 95 by 78 millimetres [3.7 by 3.1 in], 512 grams [18.1 oz]; main crystal: 31 millimetres [1.2 in] on edge)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2780M-pyrite1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Pyrite_under_Normal_and_Polarized_light.jpg/500px-Pyrite_under_Normal_and_Polarized_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Pyrite_under_Normal_and_Polarized_light.jpg/5